{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788226068724,"sessionID":"ses_fa570bfb2ffeDnSgXdchLkmauU","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"todowrite","callID":"call_bc11ffb2bf4e42afa07e5732","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"todos":[{"content":"Implement app.py (stdlib HTTP server + SQLite store + validation)","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write tests (unit + integration)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write README.md and .gitignore","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Run tests and smoke-test the API with curl","status":"pending","priority":"high"}]},"output":"[\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement app.py (stdlib HTTP server + SQLite store + validation)\",\n    \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Write tests (unit + integration)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Write README.md and .gitignore\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"medium\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Run tests and smoke-test the API with curl\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  }\n]","metadata":{"todos":[{"content":"Implement app.py (stdlib HTTP server + SQLite store + validation)","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write tests (unit + integration)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Write README.md and .gitignore","status":"pending","priority":"medium"},{"content":"Run tests and smoke-test the API with curl","status":"pending","priority":"high"}],"truncated":false},"title":"4 todos","time":{"start":1788226068696,"end":1788226068707}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The stack.json says framework: \"stdlib\". So I should use Python's standard library only — `http.server` for the web framework and `sqlite3` for the database (which is stdlib too). Let me verify whether flask got installed or not (the output was truncated). Actually, since the stack says \"stdlib\", I should stick with stdlib regardless. Let me uninstall flask if it was installed, to stay true to the constraint... Actually, it's fine to have it installed but not use it. But better to check.\n\nLet me build the API using stdlib:\n- `http.server.ThreadingHTTPServer` + `BaseHTTPRequestHandler`\n- `sqlite3` for storage\n- `json` for serialization\n- `re` for path routing\n\nRequirements:\n- POST /books — Create a book (title, author, year, isbn)\n- GET /books — List all books, with ?author= filter\n- GET /books/{id} — Get single book\n- PUT /books/{id} — Update a book\n- DELETE /books/{id} — Delete a book\n- SQLite storage\n- JSON responses, appropriate status codes\n- Validation: title and author required\n- GET /health health check\n- README.md\n- At least 3 unit/integration tests\n\nPlan:\n1. `app.py` — the main application (handler + DB layer)\n2. `tests/test_app.py` — tests using unittest (stdlib) or pytest (installed). pytest is available, so I can write pytest-style tests, but to be safe I can make them runnable with both unittest and pytest. Let me write tests using unittest.TestCase style which works with pytest too.\n3. `README.md`\n\nDesign details:\n- DB file: use a temp file or configurable via env var `BOOKS_DB` with default `books.db`. For tests, use `:memory:` or a temp file. Since ThreadingHTTPServer uses multiple threads, sqlite connections need `check_same_thread=False` or a connection per request. I'll create a connection per request — simple and thread-safe.\n- For testability, structure the app so tests can either start a real server on port 0 or call handler functions directly. Best: create a `BookStore` class encapsulating SQLite logic, and an HTTP handler that uses it. Tests can test BookStore directly (unit tests) and spin up a server for integration tests (http.client to talk to it).\n\nStatus codes:\n- POST /books: 201 Created with the created book (including id). 400 on invalid input (missing title/author), maybe 422? I'll use 400.\n- GET /books: 200 with list.\n- GET /books/{id}: 200, or 404 if not found. 400 if id is not an integer.\n- PUT /books/{id}: 200 with updated book, 404 if not found, 400 if invalid.\n- DELETE /books/{id}: 204 No Content (or 200). I'll use 204. 404 if not found.\n- GET /health: 200 {\"status\": \"ok\"}\n- Unknown route: 404.\n\nValidation: title and author required (non-empty strings). Year: optional, integer if provided. ISBN: optional string. For PUT, partial updates or full replacement? PUT typically full replacement — but I'll allow partial updates with validation that if provided, title/author can't be empty. Actually simpler and defensible: PUT accepts full or partial; validate provided fields. Hmm, \"Update a book\" — I'll implement PUT as a full replace with required title/author, but also accept partial? Let me keep it simple and consistent: PUT requires title and author (like POST), year/isbn optional. Actually, many implementations do partial updates. To be safe and simple, I'll make PUT validate title/author required (full update semantics). That matches \"title and author are required\" validation requirement.\n\nHmm, but a more flexible approach: PATCH-like partial. The task says PUT /books/{id} — Update a book. I'll go with PUT replacing title/author/year/isbn; title and author required. year/isbn optional and can be null.\n\nLet me also handle malformed JSON -> 400.\n\nYear validation: if provided and not an int, 400. Accept string digits? I'll require int (or numeric string convertible... no, keep strict: must be int if present). Actually JSON numbers parse to int. I'll accept int; reject bool (isinstance(y, bool) check). If string given, try to convert to int? Let me be lenient-ish but strict enough: if year present and not None, must be int (reject bool). 400 otherwise.\n\nISBN: optional string.\n\nNow the code:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Book collection REST API built with the Python standard library.\"\"\"\n\nimport json\nimport os\nimport re\nimport sqlite3\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer\n\nDB_PATH = os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", \"books.db\")\n\n_SCHEMA = \"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\n    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    title TEXT NOT NULL,\n    author TEXT NOT NULL,\n    year INTEGER,\n    isbn TEXT\n);\n\"\"\"\n\n\nclass BookStore:\n    \"\"\"SQLite-backed storage for books.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, db_path=DB_PATH):\n        self.db_path = db_path\n\n    def _connect(self):\n        conn = sqlite3.connect(self.db_path)\n        conn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\n        return conn\n\n    def init_schema(self):\n        with self._connect() as conn:\n            conn.execute(_SCHEMA)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _to_dict(row):\n        return {\n            \"id\": row[\"id\"],\n            \"title\": row[\"title\"],\n            \"author\": row[\"author\"],\n            \"year\": row[\"year\"],\n            \"isbn\": row[\"isbn\"],\n        }\n\n    def create(self, title, author, year=None, isbn=None):\n        with self._connect() as conn:\n            cur = conn.execute(\n                \"INSERT INTO books (title, author, year, isbn) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n                (title, author, year, isbn),\n            )\n            return self.get(cur.lastrowid, conn=conn)\n```\n\nHmm, careful with nested connections. Let me make get accept an optional connection, or just re-query with a new connection — fine since id exists after commit. Note: `with sqlite3.connect(...)` context manager commits on success but does NOT close the connection. I need to close connections to avoid file handle leaks. Let me use contextlib.closing or explicit try/finally. I'll write a small helper:\n\n```python\nfrom contextlib import closing\n\ndef create(self, ...):\n    with closing(self._connect()) as conn:\n        with conn:\n            cur = conn.execute(...)\n            row_id = cur.lastrowid\n        return self._fetch(row_id, conn)\n```\n\nActually `with conn:` commits. Then fetch in same connection. Fine.\n\nValidation function:\n\n```python\ndef validate_payload(data):\n    \"\"\"Return (clean_fields, error_message).\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        return None, \"Request body must be a JSON object.\"\n    errors = {}\n    title = data.get(\"title\")\n    author = data.get(\"author\")\n    if not isinstance(title, str) or not title.strip():\n        errors[\"title\"] = \"title is required and must be a non-empty string\"\n    ...\n```\n\nSimpler: return list of error messages, respond 400 with {\"errors\": [...]}.\n\nYear: `year = data.get(\"year\")`; if year is not None: must be int and not bool. \n\nisbn: if not None, must be str. Maybe strip strings.\n\nHTTP handler:\n\n```python\nclass BookRequestHandler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    store: BookStore = None  # injected\n\n    def do_GET(self):\n        if self.path == \"/health\": ...\n        ...\n```\n\nRouting: parse path and query with urllib.parse. Path may include query string. Use `urllib.parse.urlparse(self.path)`.\n\nRoutes:\n- GET /health -> 200 {\"status\": \"ok\"}\n- GET /books -> list with optional author filter (exact match? substring? I'll do exact match — \"?author= filter\". I'll do case-insensitive exact match? Hmm. Simple: exact match. Or case-insensitive partial? Let me do exact match to keep it predictable. Actually a filter that does substring match is friendlier. The spec just says \"support ?author= filter\". I'll implement exact match on author. Hmm... let me do case-insensitive exact match — that's a reasonable middle ground. Actually simplest defensible: `WHERE author = ?`. I'll go exact match.\n- GET /books/{id}\n- POST /books\n- PUT /books/{id}\n- DELETE /books/{id}\n\nRegex: `^/books/(\\d+)$` for id routes. If path matches `/books/...` but id isn't digits -> 400 or 404. I'll return 404 for non-integer ids... Actually 400 makes sense for malformed id. Let me return 404 — \"no book with that id\". Hmm, common practice: non-integer id -> 404 (resource not found). I'll use 404 for consistency. Eh — I'll go with 400 for invalid id format? Let me think: GET /books/abc — the URL is well-formed but \"abc\" is not a valid id. Many frameworks (FastAPI) return 422. Flask+int converter returns 404. I'll return 404. Simple.\n\nResponse helper:\n\n```python\ndef _send_json(self, status, payload):\n    body = json.dumps(payload).encode(\"utf-8\")\n    self.send_response(status)\n    self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n    self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(body)))\n    self.end_headers()\n    self.wfile.write(body)\n```\n\nFor 204: no body.\n\nReading body:\n\n```python\nlength = int(self.headers.get(\"Content-Length\") or 0)\nraw = self.rfile.read(length) if length else b\"\"\nif not raw: 400 \"request body required\"\ntry: data = json.loads(raw)\nexcept: 400 \"invalid JSON\"\n```\n\nServer factory:\n\n```python\ndef create_server(host=\"127.0.0.1\", port=8000, db_path=None):\n    store = BookStore(db_path or DB_PATH)\n    store.init_schema()\n    handler = type(\"Handler\", (BookRequestHandler,), {\"store\": store})\n    return ThreadingHTTPServer((host, port), handler)\n```\n\nActually simpler: make store a class attribute set via class variable before instantiation:\n\n```python\nclass BookRequestHandler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    store = None\n```\n\nand in create_server: `BookRequestHandler.store = store`. But mutating global class is meh for tests running multiple servers; using a subclass avoids cross-test pollution. I'll create a subclass per server.\n\nmain:\n\n```python\ndef main():\n    host = os.environ.get(\"HOST\", \"0.0.0.0\")\n    port = int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"8000\"))\n    server = create_server(host, port)\n    print(f\"Serving on http://{host}:{port}\")\n    try:\n        server.serve_forever()\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        server.server_close()\n```\n\nThreading server with sqlite: each request opens its own connection — thread-safe. Good. Enable WAL? Not needed. For test isolation, tests use temp db files or `:memory:`? `:memory:` per connection would be a fresh empty DB each time — bad. For tests via HTTP, use a temp file db. For unit tests of BookStore, also temp file (or a shared-memory... no, temp file is fine).\n\nAlternative: allow BookStore to keep a single connection with check_same_thread=False and a lock. That would let tests use \":memory:\". Hmm, that's actually clean: one connection guarded by a threading.Lock. Requests serialize on the lock, but for a test/eval service that's fine. But per-request connections are more idiomatic for concurrent servers. I'll do per-request connections and temp-file DBs in tests. For unit tests, a temp file is fine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: allowing `:memory:` complicates nothing if I do a single persistent connection with a lock. But then concurrent requests serialize... which is fine functionally. Trade-offs are minor. I'll go with per-request connections (idiomatic sqlite3 usage, thread-safe by design). Tests use tempfile.\n\nLog suppression: BaseHTTPRequestHandler logs to stderr; fine, but in tests it's noisy. Override `log_message` to no-op or keep? I'll keep minimal logging but suppress in tests by overriding? Simpler: override log_message to write only when env var BOOKS_LOG != \"0\"? Overkill. I'll just override log_message to pass (quiet). Or keep logging — tests capture stderr anyway with pytest. I'll silence it to keep output clean: `def log_message(self, format, *args): pass`. Hmm, some might want logs. README can mention. I'll silence.\n\nTests (pytest-compatible, using unittest.TestCase for stdlib-ness):\n\nTest file `tests/test_app.py`:\n\n1. Unit tests for BookStore: create/get/update/delete/list/filter.\n2. Integration tests: spin up server on port 0 in a thread, use urllib.request/http.client to make requests. Test full CRUD + validation + 404s + health.\n\nAt least 3 tests required; I'll write ~15.\n\nLet me write test helpers:\n\n```python\nimport http.client\nimport json\nimport shutil\nimport tempfile\nimport threading\nimport unittest\n\nfrom app import BookStore, create_server\n\n\nclass APITestCase(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp(self):\n        self.tmpdir = tempfile.mkdtemp()\n        self.db_path = os.path.join(self.tmpdir, \"books.db\")\n        self.server = create_server(\"127.0.0.1\", 0, self.db_path)\n        self.port = self.server.server_address[1]\n        self.thread = threading.Thread(target=self.server.serve_forever, daemon=True)\n        self.thread.start()\n\n    def tearDown(self):\n        self.server.shutdown()\n        self.server.server_close()\n        self.thread.join()\n        shutil.rmtree(self.tmpdir)\n\n    def request(self, method, path, payload=None):\n        conn = http.client.HTTPConnection(\"127.0.0.1\", self.port)\n        body = json.dumps(payload) if payload is not None else None\n        headers = {\"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"} if payload is not None else {}\n        conn.request(method, path, body=body, headers=headers)\n        resp = conn.getresponse()\n        raw = resp.read()\n        conn.close()\n        data = json.loads(raw) if raw else None\n        return resp.status, data\n```\n\nTests:\n- test_health\n- test_create_book_returns_201_with_id\n- test_create_book_missing_title_400\n- test_create_book_missing_author_400\n- test_create_book_invalid_json_400 (send raw bad body)\n- test_list_books\n- test_list_books_filter_by_author\n- test_get_book\n- test_get_book_not_found_404\n- test_update_book\n- test_update_book_validation_400\n- test_update_book_not_found_404\n- test_delete_book_204_then_404\n- test_method_not_allowed (e.g., PATCH /books -> 405)? I should add 405 handling for known paths with wrong methods. Nice touch. Let me implement: for /books and /books/{id}, allowed methods known; others -> 405 with Allow header. I'll implement in handler: parse route, then dispatch based on method; if method not in allowed set for the matched route -> 405. For unknown path -> 404.\n\nLet me structure routing:\n\n```python\ndef _route(self):\n    parsed = urlparse(self.path)\n    path = parsed.path.rstrip(\"/\") or \"/\"  # normalize? /books/ -> /books\n    if path == \"/health\":\n        return (\"health\", None)\n    if path == \"/books\":\n        return (\"books\", None)\n    m = re.fullmatch(r\"/books/([^/]+)\", path)\n    if m:\n        return (\"book\", m.group(1))\n    return (\"unknown\", None)\n```\n\nAllowed: health -> GET; books -> GET, POST; book -> GET, PUT, DELETE, (PATCH? no). unknown -> 404 regardless.\n\nDispatch in each do_METHOD:\n\n```python\ndef do_GET(self): self._dispatch(\"GET\")\ndef do_POST(self): self._dispatch(\"POST\")\ndef do_PUT(self): self._dispatch(\"PUT\")\ndef do_DELETE(self): self._dispatch(\"DELETE\")\n\ndef _dispatch(self, method):\n    route, arg = self._route()\n    if route == \"health\": ... GET only\n```\n\nBetter: table:\n\n```python\nROUTES = {\n    \"health\": {\"GET\": _handle_health},\n    \"books\": {\"GET\": _handle_list, \"POST\": _handle_create},\n    \"book\": {\"GET\": ..., \"PUT\": ..., \"DELETE\": ...},\n}\n```\n\nBound method table built in handler. Then:\n\n```python\nhandler = self.ROUTES[route].get(method)\nif route == \"unknown\" or (route == \"book\" and not valid id...) -> 404\n```\n\nFor \"book\" route, first parse id: `try: book_id = int(arg)` except -> 404. (int(\"12abc\") raises; int with digits only matches regex earlier if I use (\\d+). I'll use ([^/]+) and validate int -> 404 if invalid.)\n\nThen if method not allowed -> 405 with Allow header. Else call.\n\nAlso handle other methods not implemented (do_PATCH etc.) — BaseHTTPRequestHandler sends 501 for unimplemented methods automatically. That's acceptable.\n\nPUT handling: read body, validate (title/author required, year int, isbn str), update; if no book with id -> 404. Note: should PUT with missing title be 400 even if book doesn't exist? Either order fine; I'll validate id existence first? Hmm — for PUT, checking 404 first avoids validating body for nonexistent resource. I'll check 404 first: `existing = store.get(id)`; if None -> 404. Then validate body -> 400. Then update.\n\nFor DELETE: return 204 with empty body. Then subsequent GET -> 404.\n\nGET list: `author` query param: `parse_qs(parsed.query).get(\"author\")` — take first value. Filter exact match.\n\nAlso support `GET /books?author=` empty value -> parse_qs drops empty values by default; treat as no filter (fine).\n\nContent-Type header check on POST/PUT? Some APIs require application/json. I'll be lenient — parse body as JSON regardless. Fine.\n\nAlso add `POST /books` returning Location header? Not required. I'll include it as a nice touch — harmless.\n\nNow README.md with setup/run/test instructions and API examples.\n\nAlso requirements.txt? Stdlib only, so not needed; maybe a note. I'll skip requirements.txt since no deps. Actually a README section \"No third-party dependencies\" suffices. Maybe include pytest in README for running tests (pytest is installed). Tests written with unittest will run under pytest too.\n\nEdge: `python app.py` runs server. Also make it importable without running (`if __name__ == \"__main__\": main()`).\n\nOne more detail: ThreadingHTTPServer sets daemon_threads = True by default (it's ThreadingHTTPServer -> daemon_threads True? ThreadingHTTPServer inherits ThreadingMixIn with daemon_threads = True in http.server? Actually ThreadingHTTPServer = ThreadingMixIn + HTTPServer with daemon_threads = True). Yes, ThreadingHTTPServer sets daemon_threads = True. Good.\n\nallow_reuse_address is True on HTTPServer by default. Good for tests.\n\nFor sqlite, connections: `sqlite3.connect(path, timeout=10)`. Per-request connect overhead is fine.\n\nLet me also think about `year` accepting 0 or negative — allow any int.\n\nisbn: strip? Keep as given but require str type if present and not None.\n\nStrip title/author whitespace, require non-empty after strip.\n\nAlso reject non-dict JSON bodies (e.g., a list) -> 400.\n\nNow write the files.\n\napp.py:\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Book collection REST API built with the Python standard library.\n\nEndpoints:\n    GET    /health\n    GET    /books            (?author= filter supported)\n    POST   /books\n    GET    /books/{id}\n    PUT    /books/{id}\n    DELETE /books/{id}\n\nData is stored in SQLite. The database file location can be set with the\nBOOKS_DB environment variable (defaults to books.db next to this file).\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport json\nimport os\nimport re\nimport sqlite3\nfrom contextlib import closing\nfrom http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer\nfrom urllib.parse import parse_qs, urlparse\n\nDEFAULT_DB_PATH = os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), \"books.db\"))\n\n_SCHEMA = \"\"\"\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS books (\n    id     INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\n    title  TEXT    NOT NULL,\n    author TEXT    NOT NULL,\n    year   INTEGER,\n    isbn   TEXT\n)\n\"\"\"\n\n_BOOK_ID_PATTERN = re.compile(r\"^/books/([^/]+)$\")\n\n\nclass ValidationError(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, errors):\n        super().__init__(\"; \".join(errors))\n        self.errors = errors\n\n\ndef validate_book_payload(data, *, require_core_fields=True):\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, since PUT requires title/author anyway, a single validator with required=True always. But partial update isn't supported, so one validator:\n\n```python\ndef validate_book_payload(data):\n    \"\"\"Validate a JSON payload for create/update.\n\n    Raises ValidationError with a list of messages when the payload is bad.\n    Returns a dict with keys title, author, year, isbn.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(data, dict):\n        raise ValidationError([\"Request body must be a JSON object.\"])\n\n    errors = []\n\n    def clean_text(field, required):\n        value = data.get(field)\n        if value is None:\n            if required:\n                errors.append(f\"'{field}' is required.\")\n            return None\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            errors.append(f\"'{field}' must be a string.\")\n            return None\n        value = value.strip()\n        if required and not value:\n            errors.append(f\"'{field}' must not be empty.\")\n            return None\n        return value\n\n    title = clean_text(\"title\", True)\n    author = clean_text(\"author\", True)\n\n    year = data.get(\"year\")\n    if year is not None:\n        if isinstance(year, bool) or not isinstance(year, int):\n            errors.append(\"'year' must be an integer.\")\n\n    isbn = data.get(\"isbn\")\n    if isbn is not None and not isinstance(isbn, str):\n        errors.append(\"'isbn' must be a string.\")\n\n    if errors:\n        raise ValidationError(errors)\n\n    return {\"title\": title, \"author\": author, \"year\": year, \"isbn\": isbn}\n```\n\nWait — unknown fields? Ignore or reject? I'll ignore extra fields. Fine.\n\nBookStore:\n\n```python\nclass BookStore:\n    \"\"\"SQLite-backed storage layer. Safe to share between threads: every\n    operation opens its own connection.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, db_path=DEFAULT_DB_PATH):\n        self.db_path = db_path\n\n    def _connect(self):\n        return sqlite3.connect(self.db_path, timeout=10)\n\n    def init_schema(self):\n        with closing(self._connect()) as conn:\n            conn.execute(_SCHEMA)\n            conn.commit()\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _as_book(row):\n        if row is None:\n            return None\n        return {\"id\": row[0], \"title\": row[1], \"author\": row[2], \"year\": row[3], \"isbn\": row[4]}\n```\n\nUsing positional rows (no row_factory needed) — fine and fast.\n\n```python\n    def create(self, fields):\n        with closing(self._connect()) as conn:\n            cur = conn.execute(\n                \"INSERT INTO books (title, author, year, isbn) VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\",\n                (fields[\"title\"], fields[\"author\"], fields[\"year\"], fields[\"isbn\"]),\n            )\n            conn.commit()\n            return self._get_with(conn, cur.lastrowid)\n\n    def _get_with(self, conn, book_id):\n        row = conn.execute(\"SELECT id, title, author, year, isbn FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,)).fetchone()\n        return self._as_book(row)\n\n    def get(self, book_id):\n        with closing(self._connect()) as conn:\n            return self._get_with(conn, book_id)\n\n    def list(self, author=None):\n        query = \"SELECT id, title, author, year, isbn FROM books\"\n        params = ()\n        if author is not None:\n            query += \" WHERE author = ?\"\n            params = (author,)\n        query += \" ORDER BY id\"\n        with closing(self._connect()) as conn:\n            rows = conn.execute(query, params).fetchall()\n        return [self._as_book(r) for r in rows]\n\n    def update(self, book_id, fields):\n        with closing(self._connect()) as conn:\n            cur = conn.execute(\n                \"UPDATE books SET title = ?, author = ?, year = ?, isbn = ? WHERE id = ?\",\n                (fields[\"title\"], fields[\"author\"], fields[\"year\"], fields[\"isbn\"], book_id),\n            )\n            conn.commit()\n            if cur.rowcount == 0:\n                return None\n            return self._get_with(conn, book_id)\n\n    def delete(self, book_id):\n        with closing(self._connect()) as conn:\n            cur = conn.execute(\"DELETE FROM books WHERE id = ?\", (book_id,))\n            conn.commit()\n            return cur.rowcount > 0\n```\n\nNote: update with identical values still reports rowcount... sqlite3 rowcount for UPDATE counts matched rows even if values identical? SQLite's changes() counts rows that were actually updated — for UPDATE with same values, SQLite counts rows where the row matched the WHERE clause (it counts them even if values unchanged, I believe sqlite reports them as changed). Actually sqlite3_changes counts rows modified by the statement; for UPDATE, a row is considered modified even if the new values are the same? Let me recall: SQLite counts a row as changed \"if it was matched by the UPDATE's WHERE clause\" — yes, sqlite3_changes counts rows that the UPDATE statement touched, including rows where values didn't actually change. From SQLite docs: \"The sqlite3_changes() function returns the number of rows modified, inserted or deleted by the most recently completed statement... executing UPDATE counts each row matched by WHERE, even if set to same value.\" Hmm, actually there's nuance: \"changes to a row are counted even if the values are unchanged\" — I believe yes for UPDATE. Let me double check my memory: SQLite doc says \"The sqlite3_changes(D) interface returns the number of rows modified, inserted, or deleted by the most recently completed INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE statement... even if the row contents did not change\"? There is a caveat about triggers. I recall that UPDATE counts all matched rows regardless of value change. Yes — SQLite counts rows \"affected\" which includes matched rows for UPDATE even when assigning identical values. To be safe, I'll check existence first instead of relying on rowcount:\n\n```python\ndef update(self, book_id, fields):\n    with closing(self._connect()) as conn:\n        if self._get_with(conn, book_id) is None:\n            return None\n        conn.execute(...)\n        conn.commit()\n        return self._get_with(conn, book_id)\n```\n\nSafer. Similarly delete: check existence first? DELETE rowcount is reliable (0 rows matched -> 0). For DELETE, rowcount works. But to be consistent, check-then-act has a tiny race; irrelevant here. I'll use rowcount for delete (reliable) and existence check for update (returns updated row anyway).\n\nHmm, actually SQLite docs explicitly: \"sqlite3_changes() ... For UPDATE, rows that were matched by the WHERE clause but not actually modified (because the values didn't change) are still counted.\" I'm fairly confident it counts them. But existence check is unambiguous — go with it.\n\nHandler:\n\n```python\nclass BookAPIHandler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):\n    \"\"\"HTTP request handler. Expects a `store` class attribute (BookStore).\"\"\"\n\n    store = None\n    server_version = \"BooksAPI/1.0\"\n\n    # -- routing ---------------------------------------------------------\n    def do_GET(self):\n        self._dispatch(\"GET\")\n\n    def do_POST(self):\n        self._dispatch(\"POST\")\n\n    def do_PUT(self):\n        self._dispatch(\"PUT\")\n\n    def do_DELETE(self):\n        self._dispatch(\"DELETE\")\n\n    def _dispatch(self, method):\n        parsed = urlparse(self.path)\n        path = parsed.path\n        if path in (\"/health\",):\n            return self._route_health(method)\n        if path == \"/books\" or path == \"/books/\":\n            return self._route_books(method, parsed)\n        match = _BOOK_ID_PATTERN.match(path)\n        if match:\n            return self._route_book(method, match.group(1))\n        self._send_json(404, {\"error\": \"Not found.\"})\n```\n\nHmm wait — _BOOK_ID_PATTERN `^/books/([^/]+)$` would also match \"/books/\" ? No — `[^/]+` requires at least one non-slash char. \"/books/abc/def\" doesn't match. Good.\n\nTrailing slash handling: \"/books/\" -> treat as \"/books\". I handle explicitly. \"/books/5/\" -> won't match pattern -> 404. Acceptable.\n\n```python\n    def _route_health(self, method):\n        if method != \"GET\":\n            return self._send_405(\"GET\")\n        self._send_json(200, {\"status\": \"ok\"})\n\n    def _route_books(self, method, parsed):\n        if method == \"POST\":\n            return self._handle_create()\n        if method == \"GET\":\n            params = parse_qs(parsed.query)\n            author_values = params.get(\"author\")\n            author = author_values[0] if author_values else None\n            return self._send_json(200, self.store.list(author=author))\n        return self._send_405(\"GET, POST\")\n\n    def _route_book(self, method, raw_id):\n        try:\n            book_id = int(raw_id)\n        except ValueError:\n            return self._send_json(404, {\"error\": \"Not found.\"})\n        if method == \"GET\":\n            book = self.store.get(book_id)\n            if book is None:\n                return self._send_json(404, {\"error\": f\"Book {book_id} not found.\"})\n            return self._send_json(200, book)\n        if method == \"PUT\":\n            return self._handle_update(book_id)\n        if method == \"DELETE\":\n            return self._handle_delete(book_id)\n        return self._send_405(\"GET, PUT, DELETE\")\n```\n\nHandlers:\n\n```python\n    def _handle_create(self):\n        try:\n            fields = self._read_json_payload()\n        except _BadPayload as exc:\n            return self._send_json(400, {\"errors\": exc.errors})\n        book = self.store.create(fields)\n        self.send_response(201)\n        self.send_header(\"Location\", f\"/books/{book['id']}\")\n        self._send_json_body(201, book)  # hmm, restructure\n```\n\nLet me restructure _send_json to not send status itself... Simpler: make _send_json(status, payload, extra_headers=None).\n\n```python\n    def _send_json(self, status, payload, headers=None):\n        body = json.dumps(payload).encode(\"utf-8\")\n        self.send_response(status)\n        self.send_header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\")\n        self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(body)))\n        for name, value in (headers or {}).items():\n            self.send_header(name, value)\n        self.end_headers()\n        self.wfile.write(body)\n\n    def _send_no_content(self):\n        self.send_response(204)\n        self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", \"0\")\n        self.end_headers()\n```\n\nBody reading with helper exception:\n\n```python\nclass _BadPayload(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, errors):\n        self.errors = errors\n\n\n    def _read_json_payload(self):\n        length_header = self.headers.get(\"Content-Length\")\n        try:\n            length = int(length_header) if length_header is not None else 0\n        except ValueError:\n            length = 0\n        raw = self.rfile.read(length) if length > 0 else b\"\"\n        if not raw:\n            raise _BadPayload([\"A JSON request body is required.\"])\n        try:\n            data = json.loads(raw)\n        except (UnicodeDecodeError, json.JSONDecodeError):\n            raise _BadPayload([\"Request body must be valid JSON.\"])\n        try:\n            return validate_book_payload(data)\n        except ValidationError as exc:\n            raise _BadPayload(exc.errors)\n```\n\n_create:\n\n```python\n    def _handle_create(self):\n        try:\n            fields = self._read_json_payload()\n        except _BadPayload as exc:\n            return self._send_json(400, {\"errors\": exc.errors})\n        book = self.store.create(fields)\n        self._send_json(201, book, headers={\"Location\": f\"/books/{book['id']}\"})\n\n    def _handle_update(self, book_id):\n        if self.store.get(book_id) is None:\n            return self._send_json(404, {\"error\": f\"Book {book_id} not found.\"})\n        try:\n            fields = self._read_json_payload()\n        except _BadPayload as exc:\n            return self._send_json(400, {\"errors\": exc.errors})\n        book = self.store.update(book_id, fields)\n        self._send_json(200, book)\n\n    def _handle_delete(self, book_id):\n        if not self.store.delete(book_id):\n            return self._send_json(404, {\"error\": f\"Book {book_id} not found.\"})\n        self._send_no_content()\n\n    def _send_405(self, allow):\n        self._send_json(405, {\"error\": \"Method not allowed.\"}, headers={\"Allow\": allow})\n\n    def log_message(self, format, *args):  # noqa: A002 - signature from stdlib\n        pass  # keep test/production output quiet\n```\n\ncreate_server:\n\n```python\ndef create_server(host=\"127.0.0.1\", port=8000, db_path=None):\n    \"\"\"Create an HTTP server instance (not started).\"\"\"\n    store = BookStore(db_path if db_path is not None else DEFAULT_DB_PATH)\n    store.init_schema()\n\n    handler = type(\"BoundHandler\", (BookAPIHandler,), {\"store\": store})\n    return ThreadingHTTPServer((host, port), handler)\n\n\ndef main():\n    host = os.environ.get(\"HOST\", \"0.0.0.0\")\n    port = int(os.environ.get(\"PORT\", \"8000\"))\n    db_path = os.environ.get(\"BOOKS_DB\", DEFAULT_DB_PATH)\n    server = create_server(host, port, db_path)\n    print(f\"Books API listening on http://{host}:{port} (db: {db_path})\")\n    try:\n        server.serve_forever()\n    except KeyboardInterrupt:\n        pass\n    finally:\n        server.server_close()\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n```\n\nWait: default db path — if I default to \"books.db\" in cwd vs script dir? Using script dir keeps data with the app. But tests shouldn't touch it. DEFAULT_DB_PATH uses env BOOKS_DB or script-dir/books.db. create_server default uses DEFAULT_DB_PATH. Fine.\n\nNote about `type(\"BoundHandler\", ...)` — creating subclass per server; clean for tests with multiple servers.\n\nNow tests file. Also need `app.py` importable — tests import from app. Working dir /workspace, pytest adds rootdir? pytest inserts test file's dir (tests/) into sys.path with rootdir conftest... Actually with no conftest and rootdir auto-detection: pytest uses \"rootdir\" but import mode \"prepend\" inserts the first parent dir without __init__.py of the test file — that's tests/ itself, not /workspace. So `import app` would fail unless /workspace in sys.path. Options: add conftest.py at workspace root (pytest auto-inserts rootdir of conftest into sys.path? With rootdir conftest.py, pytest inserts conftest's dir into sys.path under prepend import mode). Yes — a conftest.py at /workspace makes /workspace importable. Or put tests next to app.py without a tests/ dir. Simplest: put test file at top level `test_app.py`? Deliverable says \"At least 3 unit/integration tests\" — location flexible. I'll create tests/test_app.py plus an empty tests/__init__.py? With __init__.py, pytest prepends /workspace (parent of package). That works: tests/__init__.py makes tests a package, pytest prepend mode inserts /workspace into sys.path. Yes: for a test module in a package, pytest walks up to the first dir without __init__.py and inserts that. So with tests/__init__.py, /workspace gets inserted and `import app` works.\n\nAlternatively simplest robust: add conftest.py at root with `sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(__file__))`. I'll do both tests/__init__.py and rely on package behavior... Actually just tests/__init__.py suffices. Hmm, but empty __init__.py plus package import mode: with \"prepend\" mode (default), for tests/test_app.py in package tests, basedir = /workspace gets prepended. Then `import app` works. I'll also run pytest to verify. To be extra safe, I'll add a minimal conftest.py at root inserting path. Both are cheap. Actually conftest.py at root alone (without __init__.py) also works because pytest inserts conftest dirs. I'll add conftest.py and skip __init__.py? Both approaches work independently; combining is harmless. I'll use conftest.py at root only, plus tests/ without __init__.py — pytest inserts rootdir... no wait, conftest dir insertion: pytest inserts the directory containing conftest.py into sys.path only if using \"prepend\" import mode — conftest.py itself is imported as a module with its dir prepended. Yes, root conftest.py gets its dir prepended to sys.path. That makes `import app` work. Good: root conftest.py with sys.path handling. Actually even simpler: default \"rootdir\" doesn't matter; I'll just do conftest.py:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport sys\n\nsys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))\n```\n\nTests: I'll write unittest-style (works with both pytest and `python -m unittest`).\n\nAlso integration via http.client.\n\nLet me also include a unit test class for BookStore and validator without server:\n\n```python\nclass BookStoreTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp(self):\n        fd, self.db_path = tempfile.mkstemp(suffix=\".db\")\n        os.close(fd)\n        self.store = BookStore(self.db_path)\n        self.store.init_schema()\n\n    def tearDown(self):\n        os.remove(self.db_path)\n\n    def test_create_and_get(self): ...\n    def test_list_filter_by_author(self): ...\n    def test_update(self): ...\n    def test_update_missing_returns_none(self): ...\n    def test_delete(self): ...\n```\n\nValidator tests:\n\n```python\nclass ValidatorTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_valid_payload(self)\n    def test_missing_title(self)\n    def test_missing_author(self)\n    def test_year_must_be_int(self)\n    def test_non_object_payload(self)\n```\n\nIntegration tests with running server as sketched.\n\nCount: well over 3.\n\nEdge on PATCH: not defined -> 501 from stdlib. Fine, not tested. 405 test: DELETE /books -> 405 with Allow.\n\nOne concern: `http.client` with 204 and Content-Length 0 — fine.\n\nAlso note: with keep-alive default in http.client (HTTP/1.1), BaseHTTPRequestHandler protocol_version defaults to HTTP/1.0, so connection closes each request — fine. Set protocol_version = \"HTTP/1.1\"? Requires accurate Content-Length — I always send it. For 204 I send Content-Length 0. HTTP/1.1 with correct Content-Length is fine and faster. But careful: for 405 on POST with unread body — if we don't read the request body before responding with HTTP/1.1 keep-alive, the connection gets out of sync. BaseHTTPRequestHandler with HTTP/1.1: if we never read rfile body, next request parsing reads leftover body bytes -> corruption. E.g., POST to a route that 405s (DELETE /books with body unread). To be safe, in _send_405 or dispatch, drain the body. Or keep protocol_version = \"HTTP/1.0\" (default) so connection closes after each response and leftover body is discarded. Simplest: leave HTTP/1.0 default. http.client handles that fine. I'll keep default protocol. Hmm, but with HTTP/1.0 and http.client reusing connections... http.client will close and reconnect as needed since response says HTTP/1.0 without keep-alive. My helper creates a new connection per request anyway. Good — keep HTTP/1.0 default.\n\nAlso `send_response` adds Server and Date headers automatically. Fine.\n\nOne more: for POST /health etc. — 405 handled.\n\nFor GET /books?author=X — parse_qs on parsed.query. Note author value URL-decoded automatically. Good.\n\nEmpty author filter `?author=` -> parse_qs drops empties (keep_blank_values=False default) -> no filter. OK.\n\nNow README.md:\n\n- Overview\n- Requirements (Python 3.10+; no third-party packages for the app; pytest for tests)\n- Run: `python app.py` (env vars HOST, PORT, BOOKS_DB)\n- Endpoints table with curl examples\n- Validation rules & status codes\n- Tests: `pytest -v` (or `python -m unittest discover`)\n- Project layout\n\nLet me now write everything, then run tests and a quick smoke test with curl (start server in background, curl endpoints, kill).\n\nFiles:\n1. /workspace/app.py\n2. /workspace/conftest.py\n3. /workspace/tests/test_app.py\n4. /workspace/README.md\n\nAlso maybe requirements-dev.txt? Not necessary; README mentions pytest. I'll skip. Actually the task says \"Make sure the code builds and tests pass\" — pytest is installed. Good.\n\nAlso .gitignore for books.db? Nice touch — add .gitignore with books.db, __pycache__, .pytest_cache. Harmless and sensible.\n\nLet me write app.py now.\n\nOne detail in validate: strip title/author; store stripped values. year must be int (not bool). isbn str.\n\nFor the response of POST: 201 + Location + body.\n\nWhat about updating a book where the id in URL doesn't exist: 404 — validated first.\n\nDELETE returns 204 empty.\n\nGET /books returns JSON array directly (not wrapped in object). Fine, \"List all books\".\n\nNow write the test file carefully.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Unit and integration tests for the Books API.\"\"\"\n\nimport http.client\nimport json\nimport os\nimport tempfile\nimport threading\nimport unittest\n\nfrom app import BookStore, ValidationError, create_server, validate_book_payload\n\n\nclass BookStoreTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def setUp(self):\n        fd, self.db_path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=\"books_test_\", suffix=\".db\")\n        os.close(fd)\n        self.store = BookStore(self.db_path)\n        self.store.init_schema()\n\n    def tearDown(self):\n        os.remove(self.db_path)\n\n    def test_create_and_get_roundtrip(self):\n        book = self.store.create({\"title\": \"Dune\", \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\", \"year\": 1965, \"isbn\": \"978-0441172719\"})\n        self.assertEqual(self.store.get(book[\"id\"]), book)\n        self.assertEqual(book[\"title\"], \"Dune\")\n\n    def test_get_missing_returns_none(self):\n        self.assertIsNone(self.store.get(999))\n\n    def test_list_filters_by_author(self):\n        self.store.create({\"title\": \"Dune\", \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\", \"year\": 1965, \"isbn\": None})\n        self.store.create({\"title\": \"1984\", \"author\": \"George Orwell\", \"year\": 1949, \"isbn\": None})\n        books = self.store.list(author=\"George Orwell\")\n        self.assertEqual([b[\"title\"] for b in books], [\"1984\"])\n        self.assertEqual(len(self.store.list()), 2)\n\n    def test_update_changes_fields(self):\n        book = self.store.create({\"title\": \"Old\", \"author\": \"A\", \"year\": 2000, \"isbn\": \"x\"})\n        updated = self.store.update(book[\"id\"], {\"title\": \"New\", \"author\": \"B\", \"year\": 2001, \"isbn\": \"y\"})\n        self.assertEqual(updated[\"title\"], \"New\")\n        self.assertEqual(updated[\"year\"], 2001)\n        self.assertEqual(self.store.get(book[\"id\"])[\"title\"], \"New\")\n\n    def test_update_missing_returns_none(self):\n        fields = {\"title\": \"T\", \"author\": \"A\", \"year\": None, \"isbn\": None}\n        self.assertIsNone(self.store.update(1234, fields))\n\n    def test_delete_removes_book(self):\n        book = self.store.create({\"title\": \"T\", \"author\": \"A\", \"year\": None, \"isbn\": None})\n        self.assertTrue(self.store.delete(book[\"id\"]))\n        self.assertIsNone(self.store.get(book[\"id\"]))\n        self.assertFalse(self.store.delete(book[\"id\"]))\n```\n\nValidatorTests:\n\n```python\nclass ValidateBookPayloadTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_valid_payload_is_cleaned(self):\n        fields = validate_book_payload({\"title\": \"  Dune \", \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\", \"year\": 1965, \"isbn\": \"123\", \"extra\": \"ignored\"})\n        self.assertEqual(fields[\"title\"], \"Dune\")\n        self.assertEqual(fields[\"year\"], 1965)\n        self.assertNotIn(\"extra\", fields)\n\n    def test_optional_fields_default_to_none(self):\n        fields = validate_book_payload({\"title\": \"T\", \"author\": \"A\"})\n        self.assertEqual(fields, {\"title\": \"T\", \"author\": \"A\", \"year\": None, \"isbn\": None})\n\n    def test_missing_title_raises(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(ValidationError) as ctx:\n            validate_book_payload({\"author\": \"A\"})\n        self.assertTrue(any(\"title\" in e for e in ctx.exception.errors))\n\n    def test_blank_title_raises(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(ValidationError):\n            validate_book_payload({\"title\": \"   \", \"author\": \"A\"})\n\n    def test_missing_author_raises(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(ValidationError) as ctx:\n            validate_book_payload({\"title\": \"T\"})\n        self.assertTrue(any(\"author\" in e for e in ctx.exception.errors))\n\n    def test_year_must_be_integer(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(ValidationError):\n            validate_book_payload({\"title\": \"T\", \"author\": \"A\", \"year\": \"1965\"})\n\n    def test_non_dict_payload_raises(self):\n        with self.assertRaises(ValidationError):\n            validate_book_payload([\"not\", \"a\", \"dict\"])\n```\n\nWait: year \"1965\" as string -> error 'year' must be an integer. But then errors raised. Good. Note in test_valid, extra field \"extra\" ignored — validate returns fixed keys so assertNotIn trivially true; fine.\n\nIntegration:\n\n```python\nclass APIIntegrationTests(unittest.TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpClass(cls):\n        cls.tmpdir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=\"books_api_test_\")\n        db_path = os.path.join(cls.tmpdir, \"books.db\")\n        cls.server = create_server(\"127.0.0.1\", 0, db_path)\n        cls.port = cls.server.server_address[1]\n        cls.thread = threading.Thread(target=cls.server.serve_forever, daemon=True)\n        cls.thread.start()\n\n    @classmethod\n    def tearDownClass(cls):\n        cls.server.shutdown()\n        cls.server.server_close()\n        cls.thread.join(timeout=5)\n        # best effort cleanup\n        for name in os.listdir(cls.tmpdir):\n            os.remove(os.path.join(cls.tmpdir, name))\n        os.rmdir(cls.tmpdir)\n\n    def request(self, method, path, payload=None, raw_body=None):\n        conn = http.client.HTTPConnection(\"127.0.0.1\", self.port, timeout=5)\n        headers = {}\n        body = None\n        if payload is not None:\n            body = json.dumps(payload)\n            headers[\"Content-Type\"] = \"application/json\"\n        elif raw_body is not None:\n            body = raw_body\n        if body is not None:\n            headers[\"Content-Length\"] = str(len(body.encode(\"utf-8\")))\n        conn.request(method, path, body=body, headers=headers)\n        response = conn.getresponse()\n        raw = response.read()\n        status = response.status\n        resp_headers = dict(response.getheaders())\n        conn.close()\n        data = json.loads(raw) if raw else None\n        return status, data, resp_headers\n```\n\nHmm http.client sets Content-Length automatically for str/bytes body. Setting it manually too could duplicate; http.client will skip adding if already present? If user sets Content-Length header explicitly, http.client respects it (it checks `_set_content_length` only when body is not None and header absent... Actually HTTPConnection.putrequest/_send_request: if body is not None and 'content-length' not in header_names, it sets it. So providing our own is fine, but simpler to let http.client handle it. I'll drop manual Content-Length.)\n\nTests:\n\n```python\n    def test_health(self):\n        status, data, _ = self.request(\"GET\", \"/health\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 200)\n        self.assertEqual(data, {\"status\": \"ok\"})\n\n    def test_create_book_returns_201_and_location(self):\n        status, data, headers = self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\", {\"title\": \"Dune\", \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\", \"year\": 1965, \"isbn\": \"978-0441172719\"})\n        self.assertEqual(status, 201)\n        self.assertEqual(data[\"title\"], \"Dune\")\n        self.assertIsInstance(data[\"id\"], int)\n        self.assertEqual(headers.get(\"Location\"), f\"/books/{data['id']}\")\n\n    def test_create_book_requires_title_and_author(self):\n        status, data, _ = self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\", {\"title\": \"No Author\"})\n        self.assertEqual(status, 400)\n        self.assertIn(\"errors\", data)\n\n        status, data, _ = self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\", {\"author\": \"No Title\"})\n        self.assertEqual(status, 400)\n\n    def test_create_book_rejects_invalid_json(self):\n        status, data, _ = self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\", raw_body=\"{not json\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 400)\n\n    def test_create_book_rejects_empty_body(self):\n        status, data, _ = self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 400)\n\n    def test_get_book_by_id(self):\n        _, created, _ = self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\", {\"title\": \"1984\", \"author\": \"George Orwell\", \"year\": 1949, \"isbn\": None})\n        status, data, _ = self.request(\"GET\", f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 200)\n        self.assertEqual(data[\"author\"], \"George Orwell\")\n\n    def test_get_missing_book_returns_404(self):\n        status, data, _ = self.request(\"GET\", \"/books/424242\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 404)\n\n    def test_list_books_and_author_filter(self):\n        self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\", {\"title\": \"Dune\", \"author\": \"Frank Herbert\", \"year\": 1965, \"isbn\": None})\n        self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\", {\"title\": \"Animal Farm\", \"author\": \"George Orwell\", \"year\": 1945, \"isbn\": None})\n\n        status, all_books, _ = self.request(\"GET\", \"/books\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 200)\n        self.assertGreaterEqual(len(all_books), 2)\n\n        status, filtered, _ = self.request(\"GET\", \"/books?author=George%20Orwell\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 200)\n        self.assertEqual([b[\"title\"] for b in filtered], [\"Animal Farm\"])\n\n    def test_update_book(self):\n        _, created, _ = self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\", {\"title\": \"Old Title\", \"author\": \"A\", \"year\": 2000, \"isbn\": None})\n        status, updated, _ = self.request(\"PUT\", f\"/books/{created['id']}\", {\"title\": \"New Title\", \"author\": \"B\", \"year\": 2001, \"isbn\": \"isbn-1\"})\n        self.assertEqual(status, 200)\n        self.assertEqual(updated[\"title\"], \"New Title\")\n        status, fetched, _ = self.request(\"GET\", f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        self.assertEqual(fetched[\"title\"], \"New Title\")\n\n    def test_update_missing_book_returns_404(self):\n        status, _, _ = self.request(\"PUT\", \"/books/424242\", {\"title\": \"T\", \"author\": \"A\"})\n        self.assertEqual(status, 404)\n\n    def test_update_book_validates_payload(self):\n        _, created, _ = self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\", {\"title\": \"T\", \"author\": \"A\"})\n        status, data, _ = self.request(\"PUT\", f\"/books/{created['id']}\", {\"title\": \"Only Title\"})\n        self.assertEqual(status, 400)\n        status, _, _ = self.request(\"PUT\", f\"/books/{created['id']}\", {\"title\": \"T\", \"author\": \"A\", \"year\": \"abc\"})\n        self.assertEqual(status, 400)\n\n    def test_delete_book(self):\n        _, created, _ = self.request(\"POST\", \"/books\", {\"title\": \"Delete Me\", \"author\": \"A\"})\n        status, data, _ = self.request(\"DELETE\", f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 204)\n        self.assertIsNone(data)\n        status, _, _ = self.request(\"GET\", f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 404)\n        status, _, _ = self.request(\"DELETE\", f\"/books/{created['id']}\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 404)\n\n    def test_unknown_path_returns_404(self):\n        status, _, _ = self.request(\"GET\", \"/nope\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 404)\n\n    def test_method_not_allowed(self):\n        status, _, headers = self.request(\"DELETE\", \"/books\")\n        self.assertEqual(status, 405)\n        self.assertIn(\"GET\", headers.get(\"Allow\", \"\"))\n```\n\nDELETE /books with no body: http.client with method DELETE and body None sends no Content-Length — server reads length 0, fine.\n\nNote: `request(\"POST\", \"/books\")` with payload None and raw_body None -> body None -> no Content-Length header. Server: length header None -> 0 -> raw empty -> _BadPayload -> 400. Good.\n\nBut wait — http.client for POST with body=None: it sends no body and no Content-Length. Server handles. Good.\n\nOne catch: in `_dispatch`, for POST/PUT when we return 405 or 404 without reading the body — connection closes after response (HTTP/1.0), so unread body is fine.\n\nAlso the health route with POST: 405. Should I drain body there? Not needed with HTTP/1.0 close semantics.\n\nNow the `_route_book` for PUT validates existence first then payload. In test_update_missing_book_returns_404, body {\"title\": \"T\", \"author\": \"A\"} valid but book missing -> 404. Good.\n\nLet me also double check `create_server` handler subclass creation and ThreadingHTTPServer import name: `from http.server import ThreadingHTTPServer` — available Python 3.7+. Good.\n\njson.JSONDecodeError subclass of ValueError; catching json.JSONDecodeError explicitly fine. raw bytes -> json.loads accepts bytes and decodes UTF-8 (raises UnicodeDecodeError on bad encoding). I catch both.\n\nAnother subtle thing: `validate_book_payload` — data dict may contain year as float like 1965.5 — reject (not int). Good.\n\n`bool` check: `isinstance(True, int)` is True, so exclude bools explicitly. Good.\n\nNow write README.md.\n\nAlso a smoke test at the end: run server in background, curl health, create, list, filter, get, put, delete, kill server. Use a temp BOOKS_DB to avoid creating books.db in workspace; but default path is script-dir/books.db — running smoke test in /workspace would create books.db; I'll set BOOKS_DB=/tmp/opencode/smoke.db. And add .gitignore containing books.db, __pycache__/, .pytest_cache/, *.pyc.\n\nWait, DEFAULT_DB_PATH evaluated at import time using env var; create_server(host, port, db_path=None) -> uses DEFAULT_DB_PATH. In main() I pass env BOOKS_DB explicitly. Good.\n\nEdge: In `create_server` signature default port=8000; tests pass 0.\n\nLet me also think about whether `int(raw_id)` accepting things like \" 5 \" or \"+5\" — int(\" 5 \") works (strips whitespace), int(\"+5\") works. URL path segment won't have spaces typically (would be %20 -> \" 5\" decoded). Rare edge, acceptable. Could enforce regex ^\\d+$ but int() with URL-decoded path is fine. Negative \"-1\" -> int works, store returns None -> 404. OK.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: `parse_qs(parsed.query)` for author with \"+\" — parse_qs decodes + as space. Good.\n\nLet me now also think about concurrent writes locking — sqlite default journal mode; concurrent inserts from different connections could hit \"database is locked\" with timeout 10s — fine for this scope.\n\nReady to write files. After that:\n1. `python -m pytest -v` (or `python -m pytest tests -v`)\n2. `python -m unittest discover -v` also verify.\n3. Smoke test with curl.\n\nAlso check git status at the end? Not committing (not asked).\n\nLet me write app.py fully now, being careful about details.\n\nOne more design point on error response shape: use {\"error\": \"...\"} for single errors and {\"errors\": [...]} for validation. Consistent enough. Maybe unify to {\"errors\": [...]} everywhere? For 404 I use {\"error\": ...}. I'll keep both but make validation always {\"errors\": [...]}. Fine.\n\nAlso for `Location` header — absolute path is acceptable per RFC 7231 (relative references allowed).\n\nWrite files now.\n\nActually one more consideration on the `_send_json` for 204 vs 405 Allow header — done.\n\nAlso `server_version = \"BooksAPI/1.0\"` nice.\n\nPotential issue: `self.send_response(status)` logs via log_request -> uses log_message which I've overridden to pass. Good, silent.\n\nconftest.py — also pytest might complain about rootdir conftest with sys.path hack: fine.\n\nAlternatively since tests/ has no __init__.py, pytest with rootdir /workspace and testpaths default; inserting conftest dir into sys.path — pytest imports conftest.py as top-level module with its dirname prepended → `import app` works.\n\nLet me write all files.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a9460c9001nRsn7TwV7Ud5rw","sessionID":"ses_fa570bfb2ffeDnSgXdchLkmauU","messageID":"msg_05a8fcb1f001PgXszm8hkAS0G2"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788226068724,"sessionID":"ses_fa570bfb2ffeDnSgXdchLkmauU","part":{"id":"prt_05a9455e9001plDXdEt5a2hBpR","messageID":"msg_05a8fcb1f001PgXszm8hkAS0G2","sessionID":"ses_fa570bfb2ffeDnSgXdchLkmauU","type":"text","text":"The task: build a Books REST API in Python (stack.json says stdlib framework), with SQLite storage, validation, health check, README, and tests. Let me set up a todo list and build it.","time":{"start":1788226065897,"end":1788226068716}}}
